Can you make this more Pythonic?

```python
def has_dup(items):
    for i in range(len(items)):
        for j in range(i + 1, len(items)):
            if items[i] == items[j]:
                return True
    return False
```

Yes, here's a tidier version with the same behaviour:

```python
def has_dup(items):
    return len(set(items)) != len(items)
```

The nested loop is O(n²). Putting the items in a set is O(n), at the cost of requiring hashable items.

